# Intentionally declares no merge driver.
#
# `docs/decisions.md merge=decisions-append` used to live here. Every decision-bearing PR
# appended a section immediately before the same tail marker in that one file, so any two of
# them conflicted on the same line; the driver (D-decisions-merge-driver, narrowed by
# D-decisions-merge-regions) resolved that collision automatically.
#
# D-decision-per-file removed the collision instead of resolving it: a decision is now its own
# file, docs/decisions/D-<slug>.md, so two decision-bearing PRs touch disjoint paths and merge
# cleanly with no driver at all. The declaration is retired rather than merely left unused, for
# two reasons:
#
#   * A GitHub merge queue — the point of the exercise — cannot register a repo-local merge
#     driver, and neither can GitHub's own merge, mergeability check or auto-merge.
#   * A declared driver that cannot start does not degrade to the no-driver baseline. Git marks
#     the path `UU` with "ours" in the worktree and *no conflict markers*, which a later
#     `git add -A` stages as resolved — silently dropping the base side of a normative spec
#     file. Keeping a declaration that only some call sites can honor keeps that failure mode
#     available for no remaining benefit.
#
# scripts/merge_decisions.py, scripts/register_decisions_driver.sh and the workflow steps that
# call them go inert without this line rather than misbehaving: sync_pr_with_base.sh tries the
# no-driver merge first and pushes nothing unless the driver is what made the merge succeed,
# which it can no longer be. Deleting that machinery is a follow-up, not a silent side effect
# of this file.
